/*---------------------------------------------------------------------------*\
  outerDict -- STAGE D ONLY.  THIS IS WHERE THE BED VOLUME IS COMPUTED.

  The PFR is a RATING model: it integrates the volume it is given.  Stage D's
  claim -- "a bed volume the ENGINE computed" -- is therefore the answer of
  this DesignSpec, a Newton on one case variable against one target:

      $V_bed  ->  converter.approach_K  =  5 K

  THE TARGET is the same source and the same number stage C declares, read
  the way the source itself defines it PER BED.  US 5,352,428 (Bhakta &
  Grotz, CF Braun and Co, 4 October 1994), verbatim: "The effluent from each
  bed has an approach to equilibrium of 5 C., i.e., the ammonia concentration
  corresponds to the equilibrium concentration at a temperature 5 C. above
  the actual temperature."  Stage C IMPOSED that number on an isothermal
  Gibbs converter; here the engine SOLVES for the bed volume at which an
  adiabatic kinetic bed's effluent actually reaches it.  `approach_K` is the
  PFR's own KPI: T* - T_out, where T* is the temperature at which the outlet
  gas would be at equilibrium under the rate law's own Ka (Gillespie &
  Beattie, Eq 2) -- NOT the package's Gibbs surface; the README states how far
  the two equilibria sit apart.

  WHY NOT STAGE C's 46.60 % PER-PASS CONVERSION AS THE TARGET.  It is not
  reachable by this bed at any volume: a single ADIABATIC bed's conversion is
  capped where its adiabatic line crosses the retreating equilibrium curve,
  and that cap is far below an isothermal 700 K converter's.  The README
  measures the cap with a volume sweep.  A DesignSpec aimed at an unreachable
  value diverges; aiming it there and reporting the divergence would be
  honest but would size nothing.  The sourced, reachable, per-bed criterion
  is the one above.

  WHY ONE UNKNOWN AND NOT TWO.  The bed's inlet temperature is landed by the
  preheater's own inverse solve (`phaseChanger { outletT 700 K; }` returns
  its duty), so it is not an outer unknown -- and it could not be one: a
  fixed-duty `heater` in front of this bed makes the recycle open-loop
  unstable (measured; see the flowsheetDict header), so an outer Newton
  varying that duty would have every inner evaluation run away from the
  answer it was asked to hold still.

  WHAT DOES NOT RUN UNDER THIS DRIVER, said plainly: `system/postDict` is
  NOT applied to the representative pass (the sizing/costing chain is Layer
  3 of the single-pass path only), so this case carries no postDict, no
  vessel sheet and no cost.  Stage D sizes the BED; the vessel around it
  stays stage C's, and a cost of stage D's converter would have to be built
  from this run's own V_bed in a case of its own.  The balance REPORTS do run
  on the representative pass (choupoSolve/main.cpp, Layer 3b), so the plant
  first law and mass closure below are checked -- on that pass alone.
\*---------------------------------------------------------------------------*/

type         designSpec;

manipulate
(
    //  Bounds are advisory clips on the Newton step, not physics: the lower
    //  bound keeps the FD Jacobian off a bed so short that the approach is
    //  hundreds of kelvin and the residual flat.
    { variable V_bed;   initial 6.0;    min 1.0;   max 60.0; }    // m3 of catalyst bed
);

targets
(
    { path converter.approach_K;   value 5.0;   tol 0.02; }   // K, US 5,352,428 per-bed approach
);

options
{
    maxIter      40;
    tolF         1.0;        // |F|_2 < 1: the residual within its own tol
    fdStep       1.0e-3;
}

report
{
    file     designspec_history.csv;
}
